<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chanyu</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chanyu was the title of the supreme ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu Empire</a>, roughly equivalent to emperor. The chanyu presided over a hierarchy of kings and tribal leaders and conducted relations with Han <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modu-chanyu">Modu Chanyu</a>, who reigned from 209 to 174 BCE, unified and organised the Xiongnu state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chanyu.jpg" alt="Chanyu" width="195" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Davide Mauro · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Reconstruction_of_a_Xiongnu_chief_warrior,_2nd_century_BCE-1st_century_CE.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Chanyu, also written Shanyu, was the title of the supreme rulers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-asia">Inner Asian</a> nomads for eight centuries, until it was superseded by the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">Khagan</a> in 402 CE. It is short for Chengli Gutu Chanyu. The ruling Luandi clan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> used it most widely, in the time of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin dynasty</a> (221 to 206 BCE) and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> (206 BCE to 220 CE). The Chinese later also used it, infrequently, as a name for Tujue leaders. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-han">Book of Han</a> explains the words. According to it, the Xiongnu called heaven chengli and a child gutu, while chanyu meant a vast and great appearance.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Scholars have proposed other origins for the title. L. Rogers and Edwin G. Pulleyblank argue that chanyu may be equivalent to the later title tarkhan, with an original Chinese pronunciation approximating the sound darxan. Bailey derives it from a Proto-Iranian root meaning to speak or command, and compares a Saka title in which the meaning shifted in the same way. Related forms that have been compared include the Khotanese ttarkana and the Ossetian tærxon.</p><p>Alexander Vovin tentatively proposes a Yeniseian origin for the full title, built from four roots meaning heaven, son or child, north or the lower reaches of the Yenisei, and prince, so that the whole would mean <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/son-of-heaven">Son of Heaven</a>, Ruler of the North. Dybo instead derives it from a Turkic root meaning vast as the sky, and compares the Old Uyghur words tarkan and tarkar. Her Old Uyghur tarkan is not listed in Wilkens (2021), and Caferoğlu (1968) glosses it as to feel embarrassed or to get tired of, so that this comparison is disputed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chanyu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Han court often installed southern <b>chanyus</b> who were partial to its interests, and in the late 2nd century the chanyu's own followers killed him for leading them into the Han's internal wars.</blockquote><a href="/empire/southern-xiongnu">Southern Xiongnu · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The Xianbei emerged from the ruin of the Donghu, the 'Eastern Hu', a nomadic confederation of the eastern steppe that Modu, <b>chanyu</b> of the Xiongnu, destroyed around 206 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/xianbei">Xianbei · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 200 BCE the Xiongnu trapped the Han founder, Emperor Gaozu, for seven days at Baideng; he escaped only after his envoys bribed the <b>chanyu</b>'s consort.</blockquote><a href="/empire/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The Xiongnu turned the skull of a defeated Yuezhi king into a drinking cup; according to the Han shu, Han envoys later drank from it when swearing a blood oath with a Xiongnu <b>chanyu</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/yuezhi">Yuezhi · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>A nomadic confederation united the herding peoples of the Eurasian steppe under one ruling clan and its supreme ruler, called the <b>chanyu</b> by the Xiongnu and the khagan or khan by the Turks and Mongols.</blockquote><a href="/government/nomadic-confederation">Nomadic confederation · How It Works</a></li><li><blockquote>In 36 BCE it sheltered the Xiongnu <b>chanyu</b> Zhizhi, whom a Han army killed there.</blockquote><a href="/state/kangju">Kangju</a></li></ul></section>
